<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=potato">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potato</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The potato is a starchy tuber native to the Andes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, spread worldwide after the Spanish conquest. As part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, it transformed diets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. New World crops helped support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>'s large population growth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potato.jpg" alt="Potato" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amédée Masclef · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:234_Solanum_tuberosum_L.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Families farmed small terraced fields of rye, barley and later <b>potatoes</b>, cut hay for the winter and, above all, herded sheep and cattle on high summer pastures, moving flocks seasonally to the lowlands of Catalonia and southern France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>About 54.9 percent of the land is agricultural, growing grain, cotton, tobacco, <b>potatoes</b> and sugar beet, with wine, livestock and a Caspian sturgeon fishery; tourism, damaged by the war of the 1990s, recovered in the 2000s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the island developed the Jersey breed of dairy cattle, kept pure by a ban on imported cattle from 1789, early <b>potatoes</b> such as the Jersey Royal, and tourism, while Saint Helier grew with English settlers and retired officers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people grew <b>potatoes</b> and other highland crops, and in the later phases ate mostly the meat of llamas and alpacas, much of it apparently brought from the higher grasslands as dried meat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chavin">Chavín · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Rural poverty, the <b>potato</b> failures and the hunger crisis of 1846–47, and the plight of the Silesian weavers, whose revolt was crushed in 1844, drove emigration, above all to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture combined maize grown on irrigated terraces with <b>potatoes</b>, quinoa and other crops of the high plateau, alongside herds of llamas and alpacas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>8000–5000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Lake Titicaca region, Peru and Bolivia</dd></div></dl><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/anden">Andean terraces<span>by 2000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=potato">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
